	abstract = {Christianity has seen sexuality as something that profanes humanity and has the potential to place a person in spiritual peril. The Church has maintained that sexuality can only be sanctioned with the intentional act of reproduction, and even then, only within marriage. Notorious libertine and self-confessed atheist the Marquis de Sade (who authored the repeatedly banned 120 Days of Sodom, among other novels) unsurprisingly presents a radically different view of sexuality. In his libertine novels, works that are not without philosophical significance, Sade's characters frequently espouse a hatred of reproduction, and argue vehemently for its ejection from the realm of the erotic. It was no doubt Sade's intention to place himself in direct opposition with those traditional Church views, to take up the role of sinner and antagonist. Yet, in his transgressive erotic paradigm, sexuality is recalled to the sacred, redeemed in a way that no act sanctioned by holy matrimony could be. Sade's works were a great influence on early twentieth-century thinker and writer Georges Bataille, who believes that the erotic can only affirm the inner life of man and, as such, is sacred. Bataille raises the erotic to a high status when he writes that `sexuality, thought of as filthy or beastly, is still the greatest barrier to the reduction of man to the level of thing.' Interestingly, Bataille's idea of the sacred excludes the productive and the reproductive, because he sees the sacred as the realm of the excessive, the realm of the transgressive. In order to understand and explore what Bataille suggests is the significance of this transgressive, sacred erotic for human subjectivity, this chapter will explore Sade's anti-procreative stance in the light of Bataille's theory, interrogating the implications of a principle of erotic excess.}}

